1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,302
Delhi, India.

2
00:00:01,302 --> 00:00:07,338
In the courtyard of a ruined mosque, a dark iron column stands seven meters tall.

3
00:00:07,338 --> 00:00:11,362
It has been out in the open for sixteen hundred years.

4
00:00:11,362 --> 00:00:14,566
Monsoons, heat, and the smog of a megacity.

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,688
And the internet says it never rusted.

6
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:18,526
Not a flake.

7
00:00:18,526 --> 00:00:21,880
Some say ancient smiths knew a lost super-metal.

8
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:25,933
Others say the metal did not come from this planet at all.

9
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:31,313
Tonight we handle it like an insurance adjuster handles a warranty.

10
00:00:31,313 --> 00:00:34,759
Sixteen hundred years, no rust, no maintenance.

11
00:00:34,759 --> 00:00:39,600
A claim that big needs paperwork, and every clause gets inspected.

12
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:40,731
Approved.

13
00:00:40,731 --> 00:00:41,248
Denied.

14
00:00:41,248 --> 00:00:43,315
Or approved with conditions.

15
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:50,700
No opinions and no mood, only what the laboratory, the inscription, and the iron itself can back up.

16
00:00:51,166 --> 00:01:00,376
And by the end, you will know exactly why this pillar survived, and why the real answer is a better story than the one with the aliens.

17
00:01:00,376 --> 00:01:01,399
File the claim.

18
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:06,515
Item one.

19
00:01:06,515 --> 00:01:07,019
Intake.

20
00:01:07,019 --> 00:01:15,089
The pillar is seven point two one meters tall, about forty centimeters wide, and it weighs more than six tonnes.

21
00:01:15,089 --> 00:01:17,900
More than a meter of it is underground.

22
00:01:18,366 --> 00:01:23,055
It stands in the Qutb complex, in Mehrauli, in the south of Delhi.

23
00:01:23,055 --> 00:01:28,880
Right beside it, a minaret and the ruins of a mosque built in the eleven hundreds.

24
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:31,366
The pillar is older than all of it.

25
00:01:31,833 --> 00:01:37,086
At the top, a carved bell-shaped capital, about thirty centimeters tall.

26
00:01:37,086 --> 00:01:43,799
The shaft below it is smooth, dark, and almost black, with a faint shine like old gun metal.

27
00:01:44,266 --> 00:01:46,843
Millions of people have come to see it.

28
00:01:46,843 --> 00:01:50,411
And the claim they carry with them is always the same.

29
00:01:50,411 --> 00:01:52,195
This iron has never rusted.

30
00:01:52,195 --> 00:01:55,366
So that is clause one, and it is where we begin.

31
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:56,543
Clause one.

32
00:01:56,543 --> 00:01:58,221
Does it really never rust?

33
00:01:58,221 --> 00:01:59,900
Let's inspect the surface.

34
00:02:00,366 --> 00:02:01,144
Clause one.

35
00:02:01,144 --> 00:02:02,769
The pillar never rusts.

36
00:02:02,769 --> 00:02:04,112
First, the surface.

37
00:02:04,112 --> 00:02:07,786
Look closely and you do not see bare, shining metal.

38
00:02:07,786 --> 00:02:12,733
You see a dark skin of corrosion, thin and smooth, covering all of it.

39
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:15,229
This is the first correction.

40
00:02:15,229 --> 00:02:16,699
The pillar does rust.

41
00:02:16,699 --> 00:02:19,008
Every iron object rusts a little.

42
00:02:19,008 --> 00:02:25,166
What it does not do is what ordinary iron does, which is keep rusting until it crumbles.

43
00:02:25,633 --> 00:02:32,094
Ordinary iron makes a loose, flaky rust that holds water and lets the damage move deeper.

44
00:02:32,094 --> 00:02:37,466
A little rain, a little oxygen, and a few decades later, the post is gone.

45
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:40,025
The pillar's skin is different.

46
00:02:40,025 --> 00:02:45,828
Under the microscope it is compact, tightly stuck to the metal, and almost waterproof.

47
00:02:45,828 --> 00:02:51,700
In sixteen hundred years, that film has grown only about one twentieth of a millimeter.

48
00:02:52,166 --> 00:03:03,851
That figure comes from the work of Professor R Balasubramaniam of the Indian Institute of Technology, Kanpur, who spent years studying the rust of the pillar.

49
00:03:03,851 --> 00:03:05,699
We will keep meeting him.

50
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:07,811
There is also a tradition.

51
00:03:07,811 --> 00:03:14,390
For generations, visitors stood with their backs to the pillar and tried to clasp their hands behind it.

52
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:17,300
If your fingers met, you would have good luck.

53
00:03:17,766 --> 00:03:27,087
In nineteen ninety-seven, India's Archaeological Survey built an iron grill around the pillar to protect it from all those hands.

54
00:03:27,087 --> 00:03:30,699
And that gave scientists an accidental experiment.

55
00:03:31,166 --> 00:03:36,033
The rubbing had polished a patch about five feet up until it was bright.

56
00:03:36,033 --> 00:03:41,170
Within a year of the fence, that bright patch had turned a thin, dark brown.

57
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:43,333
Fresh iron, rebuilding its skin.

58
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,389
Verdict on clause one.

59
00:03:45,389 --> 00:03:48,569
Denied as written, approved with conditions.

60
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:52,760
It rusts, slowly, and then the rust stops being a problem.

61
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:54,566
The real question is how.

62
00:03:55,033 --> 00:03:55,748
Clause two.

63
00:03:55,748 --> 00:03:57,375
Nobody knows who made it.

64
00:03:57,375 --> 00:04:02,255
Most of the claims say it is a mystery, but the pillar is signed, in a way.

65
00:04:02,255 --> 00:04:05,833
On the shaft, near the bottom, there is an inscription.

66
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:10,843
A short poem in Sanskrit, cut into the iron in Gupta-era script.

67
00:04:10,843 --> 00:04:15,529
The letters are about a centimeter tall, and they are still sharp.

68
00:04:15,529 --> 00:04:17,233
The iron kept them safe.

69
00:04:17,699 --> 00:04:20,327
The text praises a king named Chandra.

70
00:04:20,327 --> 00:04:29,732
It says he conquered enemies in Bengal in the east, that his fame reached the southern ocean, and that he was devoted to the god Vishnu.

71
00:04:29,732 --> 00:04:31,600
It never names his dynasty.

72
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:39,728
Scholars match him to Chandragupta the Second, who ruled around three seventy-five to four fifteen C E.

73
00:04:39,728 --> 00:04:43,968
His gold coins show an archer and the short name Chandra.

74
00:04:43,968 --> 00:04:47,166
The script and the poetry fit the same age.

75
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:56,722
An East India Company officer made a copy of the text in eighteen thirty-one, and a first reading was published in eighteen thirty-four.

76
00:04:56,722 --> 00:05:01,399
It took until eighteen seventy-five to read the king's name correctly.

77
00:05:01,866 --> 00:05:07,048
The inscription says the pillar was raised on a hill called Vishnupada.

78
00:05:07,048 --> 00:05:16,899
Researchers think that was Udayagiri, a hill of rock-cut caves in central India, where Chandragupta's own inscriptions have been found.

79
00:05:17,366 --> 00:05:19,500
So how did it end up in Delhi?

80
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:21,279
Honestly, we do not know.

81
00:05:21,279 --> 00:05:26,117
One view says a king named Anangpal moved it in the eleven hundreds.

82
00:05:26,117 --> 00:05:32,733
Another says the sultan Iltutmish carried it off after sacking Vidisha, around twelve thirty.

83
00:05:33,199 --> 00:05:38,378
Whoever did it moved over six tonnes of iron across hundreds of kilometers.

84
00:05:38,378 --> 00:05:44,800
If it was a trophy, it was also a message: the flagpole of your god now stands in our mosque.

85
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:46,858
Verdict on clause two.

86
00:05:46,858 --> 00:05:47,510
Approved.

87
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:51,490
A real king, a real date range, and a real inscription.

88
00:05:51,490 --> 00:05:54,458
The pillar is not an orphan from nowhere.

89
00:05:54,458 --> 00:05:55,833
It has a signature.

90
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:57,127
Clause three.

91
00:05:57,127 --> 00:06:01,519
Nobody could have built something this big sixteen hundred years ago.

92
00:06:01,519 --> 00:06:02,601
We can test that.

93
00:06:02,601 --> 00:06:06,166
Because the pillar carries the marks of how it was made.

94
00:06:06,633 --> 00:06:09,162
Ancient Indian iron was not poured.

95
00:06:09,162 --> 00:06:15,881
It was made in small clay furnaces, where ore and charcoal made a spongy lump called a bloom.

96
00:06:15,881 --> 00:06:19,566
Each lump weighed maybe twenty or thirty kilograms.

97
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:27,267
To build something bigger, you joined lumps while they were white hot, and hammered them until they fused into one.

98
00:06:27,267 --> 00:06:28,651
That is forge welding.

99
00:06:28,651 --> 00:06:31,733
And this pillar is made of that, lump after lump.

100
00:06:32,199 --> 00:06:39,847
Metallurgists believe it was built lying on its side, with discs of hot iron added from the side and hammered in.

101
00:06:39,847 --> 00:06:45,600
The joints still leave traces on the shaft, and they are where the darker rust shows.

102
00:06:46,066 --> 00:06:51,182
Those joints rust a little darker, which is why researchers know they exist.

103
00:06:51,182 --> 00:06:53,606
A solid casting would not show them.

104
00:06:53,606 --> 00:06:59,866
Even the surface was hammered smooth, and that work pressed slag-rich metal into a hard skin.

105
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:01,639
It was a huge job.

106
00:07:01,639 --> 00:07:06,138
It took a team, a lot of charcoal, and an organised workforce.

107
00:07:06,138 --> 00:07:07,299
Remarkable, yes.

108
00:07:07,299 --> 00:07:13,466
But it is exactly the kind of organised effort that empires of that age were good at.

109
00:07:13,933 --> 00:07:15,553
And Delhi is not alone.

110
00:07:15,553 --> 00:07:25,062
At Dhar, in central India, lie the broken pieces of another iron pillar, of uncertain date, perhaps the eleventh to thirteenth century.

111
00:07:25,062 --> 00:07:28,866
Together the pieces measure more than thirteen meters.

112
00:07:29,333 --> 00:07:34,747
That is about twice the height of Delhi's, and heavier, at around seven tonnes.

113
00:07:34,747 --> 00:07:38,790
It was probably the largest forged iron pillar of its time.

114
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:40,366
It was never a one-off.

115
00:07:40,366 --> 00:07:41,599
The skill existed.

116
00:07:42,066 --> 00:07:43,766
Verdict on clause three.

117
00:07:43,766 --> 00:07:44,262
Denied.

118
00:07:44,262 --> 00:07:51,487
Ancient smiths could and did forge massive iron, and the pillar still wears the marks of their method.

119
00:07:51,487 --> 00:07:53,399
The how is not the mystery.

120
00:07:53,866 --> 00:07:54,760
Clause four.

121
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:58,934
The metal itself has a secret chemistry, a lost formula.

122
00:07:58,934 --> 00:08:01,989
Here, at last, the claim has a real core.

123
00:08:01,989 --> 00:08:06,833
Because the iron is unusual, and the lab can tell us exactly how.

124
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:11,733
The pillar is almost pure wrought iron, with very little carbon.

125
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:14,711
But it has an unusual amount of phosphorus.

126
00:08:14,711 --> 00:08:16,859
About a quarter of one percent.

127
00:08:16,859 --> 00:08:20,600
Modern iron usually carries less than a fifth of that.

128
00:08:21,066 --> 00:08:21,342
Why?

129
00:08:21,342 --> 00:08:26,928
In a modern furnace, limestone is added to carry the phosphorus away in the slag.

130
00:08:26,928 --> 00:08:29,755
The ancient smiths did not use limestone.

131
00:08:29,755 --> 00:08:34,100
They used wood and ore, and the phosphorus stayed in the metal.

132
00:08:34,566 --> 00:08:37,226
Some researchers add another detail.

133
00:08:37,226 --> 00:08:44,690
The fuel may have included phosphorus-rich woods, such as Cassia auriculata, a common shrub in India.

134
00:08:44,690 --> 00:08:48,533
Charcoal from it would push even more phosphorus in.

135
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:54,623
The iron also holds tiny specks of slag, glassy leftovers from the smelting.

136
00:08:54,623 --> 00:08:56,768
Normally, impurities are bad.

137
00:08:56,768 --> 00:09:01,799
Here, they turn out to matter, because they change how rust behaves.

138
00:09:02,266 --> 00:09:05,566
Balasubramaniam's team proposed the sequence.

139
00:09:05,566 --> 00:09:07,766
At first, ordinary rust forms.

140
00:09:07,766 --> 00:09:16,933
But the slag specks and the phosphorus stack up at the boundary between metal and rust, and a different layer begins to grow.

141
00:09:17,399 --> 00:09:20,646
First an amorphous layer called misawite.

142
00:09:20,646 --> 00:09:26,189
Then, slowly, a thin crystal layer of iron hydrogen phosphate hydrate.

143
00:09:26,189 --> 00:09:31,178
That crystal layer is dense and it hardly lets in water or air.

144
00:09:31,178 --> 00:09:33,000
Rust has nowhere to go.

145
00:09:33,466 --> 00:09:35,490
It needs help from the weather.

146
00:09:35,490 --> 00:09:39,993
Alternate wetting and drying makes that phosphate crystal layer form.

147
00:09:39,993 --> 00:09:42,212
It cannot be switched on in a day.

148
00:09:42,212 --> 00:09:45,933
It took years, even decades, and it keeps getting better.

149
00:09:46,399 --> 00:09:48,016
Verdict on clause four.

150
00:09:48,016 --> 00:09:49,773
Approved with conditions.

151
00:09:49,773 --> 00:09:54,271
The chemistry is real, but it is not lost, and it is not secret.

152
00:09:54,271 --> 00:09:57,996
It came from local ore, local wood, and no limestone.

153
00:09:57,996 --> 00:10:00,666
They did not need to know the formula.

154
00:10:01,133 --> 00:10:01,992
Clause five.

155
00:10:01,992 --> 00:10:03,353
The weather did it.

156
00:10:03,353 --> 00:10:08,869
An older theory says the iron is ordinary, and Delhi's climate is the reason.

157
00:10:08,869 --> 00:10:14,599
In nineteen seventy, a Swedish corrosion scientist, Wranglen, said exactly that.

158
00:10:15,066 --> 00:10:20,188
Rust runs faster when the air stays above about seventy percent humidity.

159
00:10:20,188 --> 00:10:23,275
Delhi, for most of the year, stays below it.

160
00:10:23,275 --> 00:10:28,466
Dry air really does slow iron down, and the pillar stands in the open air.

161
00:10:28,933 --> 00:10:31,841
But the weather cannot be the whole story.

162
00:10:31,841 --> 00:10:35,994
Look at Konark, on the Odisha coast, with sea salt and damp.

163
00:10:35,994 --> 00:10:40,079
Its temple uses big iron beams from the thirteenth century.

164
00:10:40,079 --> 00:10:41,533
They are still there.

165
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:50,437
Or Kollur, in the humid hills of the west coast, where an old iron pillar stands at a temple in heavy monsoon rain.

166
00:10:50,437 --> 00:10:52,711
Same family of phosphoric iron.

167
00:10:52,711 --> 00:10:54,399
Very different weather.

168
00:10:54,399 --> 00:10:55,500
Still standing.

169
00:10:55,966 --> 00:10:58,352
So, climate and metal work together.

170
00:10:58,352 --> 00:11:03,258
The dry air helps the pillar, and the phosphoric iron helps it everywhere.

171
00:11:03,258 --> 00:11:07,566
Neither explains the pillar alone, and neither needs a spaceship.

172
00:11:08,033 --> 00:11:11,153
There is one more catch for the miracle story.

173
00:11:11,153 --> 00:11:19,497
Researchers could not cut a piece from the pillar, so some of the lab work used rust flakes, and old Gupta-era iron clamps.

174
00:11:19,497 --> 00:11:21,600
The pillar itself is protected.

175
00:11:22,066 --> 00:11:23,721
Verdict on clause five.

176
00:11:23,721 --> 00:11:25,520
Approved with conditions.

177
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:28,182
The climate is a helper, not a trick.

178
00:11:28,182 --> 00:11:35,233
It explains why Delhi is a lucky home, and why the damp coast iron beams are even more impressive.

179
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:36,521
Clause six.

180
00:11:36,521 --> 00:11:38,313
Extraterrestrial origin.

181
00:11:38,313 --> 00:11:40,703
This one has a traceable author.

182
00:11:40,703 --> 00:11:49,366
In nineteen sixty-eight, Erich von Daniken wrote Chariots of the Gods, and used the pillar as a proof of alien help.

183
00:11:49,833 --> 00:11:59,748
In nineteen seventy-four, an interviewer from Playboy told him that the column was not rust-free, and that its method of manufacture was understood.

184
00:11:59,748 --> 00:12:03,566
His answer: he no longer considered the pillar a mystery.

185
00:12:04,033 --> 00:12:06,468
One more mark deserves a mention.

186
00:12:06,468 --> 00:12:11,485
About four meters up the shaft is a deep dent, made by a cannonball.

187
00:12:11,485 --> 00:12:18,200
Historians think it was fired in seventeen thirty-nine, probably by the army of Nadir Shah.

188
00:12:18,666 --> 00:12:23,231
The ball cracked the metal on the far side, and the pillar still stood.

189
00:12:23,231 --> 00:12:25,482
That is a stronger story than rust.

190
00:12:25,482 --> 00:12:29,533
Whatever this iron is, it took a cannon, and it stayed upright.

191
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,635
Verdict on clause six.

192
00:12:31,635 --> 00:12:32,155
Denied.

193
00:12:32,155 --> 00:12:35,574
Its main advocate withdrew it fifty years ago.

194
00:12:35,574 --> 00:12:40,999
Meanwhile, every question that he used it for now has a published answer.

195
00:12:41,466 --> 00:12:42,099
Case file.

196
00:12:42,099 --> 00:12:44,566
Time to score the Iron Pillar of Delhi.

197
00:12:45,033 --> 00:12:49,125
A Gupta-era inscription naming King Chandra: evidence.

198
00:12:49,125 --> 00:12:53,369
Forge-welded wrought iron, high in phosphorus: evidence.

199
00:12:53,369 --> 00:12:58,750
A thin crystal phosphate film, one twentieth of a millimeter: evidence.

200
00:12:58,750 --> 00:13:01,933
Delhi's dry climate as a helper: evidence.

201
00:13:02,399 --> 00:13:05,130
Never rusts: false, it rusts slowly.

202
00:13:05,130 --> 00:13:08,847
A lost super-metal: no, local ore and local wood.

203
00:13:08,847 --> 00:13:12,033
An alien alloy: dropped by its own author.

204
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:13,407
The verdict.

205
00:13:13,407 --> 00:13:15,221
Human genius, not alien.

206
00:13:15,221 --> 00:13:22,404
A sixteen-hundred-year warranty honoured by phosphorus, slag, dry air, and a skilled workforce.

207
00:13:22,404 --> 00:13:23,160
Not magic.

208
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:24,899
Not an accident either.

209
00:13:25,366 --> 00:13:28,622
And here is the part the aliens cannot match.

210
00:13:28,622 --> 00:13:31,734
This tradition did not die with the Guptas.

211
00:13:31,734 --> 00:13:40,200
Smiths in India kept making phosphoric iron for centuries, and Dhar, Konark, and Kollur all carry the same signature.

212
00:13:40,666 --> 00:13:42,980
It is a monument to patient craft.

213
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:53,666
People who never heard of phosphates built a pillar that outlasted empires, and it is still standing because they paid attention to what their materials did.

214
00:13:54,133 --> 00:13:57,826
Next case: a stone lid in a Maya tomb at Palenque.

215
00:13:57,826 --> 00:14:03,144
An astronaut at the controls of a rocket, or the world tree of the Maya?

216
00:14:03,144 --> 00:14:04,400
We open the tomb.

217
00:14:04,866 --> 00:14:07,725
Did you ever hear the rustless pillar story?

218
00:14:07,725 --> 00:14:13,766
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery, tested clause by clause.
